---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **차트 어디에 앉아 있나.** (`/rank-shape`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **줄세우지 않는다.** 시장을 「잘 나가는 순」으로 안 놓는다. 몫으로 띠를 갈라 **나란히** 놓고
 *    왜 모양이 다른지를 같이 적는다.
 * ⛔ **「한 작품이 끌었나」를 표 안에** 넣는다. 뒤에 각주로 숨기지 않는다.
 * ⛔ **영화와 시리즈를 갈라서도** 낸다. 합친 표만 보고 쓰면 한쪽이 모양을 지배해도 안 보인다.
 * ⛔ 「한국 작품이 1위감이 아니다」라 쓰지 않는다. 순위는 **결과**지 까닭이 아니다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-rank-shape.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-rank-shape.json';

const 아래 = data.bands[0];
const 중1 = data.bands[1];
const 중2 = data.bands[2];
const 위 = data.bands[3];

const TITLE = 'Where on the chart do Korean titles sit?';
const DESC =
  `Across ${data.markets} Netflix markets, Korean titles hold ${data.overallPc}% of all chart places. `
  + `In the ${중1.markets} markets where their share is ${중1.label}, they hold only ${중1.rank1Pc}% of `
  + `number-one places — below their ${중1.overallPc}% overall. In the ${위.markets} markets where they `
  + `are strongest, it runs the other way: ${위.rank1Pc}% at number one against ${위.overallPc}% overall.`;

/** 막대 길이 — 띠마다 그 띠의 가장 큰 칸을 100 으로 놓는다. 높낮이가 아니라 **모양**을 보이려는 것 */
const 막대 = (띠) => {
  const 최대 = Math.max(...띠.byRank.map((x) => x.pc));
  return 띠.byRank.map((x) => ({ ...x, w: Math.round((100 * x.pc) / 최대) }));
};

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/rank-shape">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every page we publish counts chart <i>places</i>. A place at number one and a place at number ten
    both count as one. This page asks whether they arrive in equal numbers — and they do not.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>Added up, the answer is no answer</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      Across all {data.markets} markets and all {data.ranks} ranks, Korean titles hold
      {' '}<b>{data.rank1Pc}%</b> of number-one places and <b>{data.rank10Pc}%</b> of number-ten
      places, against <b>{data.overallPc}%</b> of places overall —
      {' '}{data.marketsBelowAtOne} of {data.markets} markets sit lower at number one than
      across their chart. That is close enough to a coin toss to be worth nothing.
      <b> The world total is where this question goes to die</b>, and it is the figure we would have
      published if we had stopped there.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      It only separates once the markets are grouped by how much of the chart Korean titles hold to
      begin with — and then it separates in two opposite directions at once, which is why the total
      is flat.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The shape depends on how much of the chart Korean titles already hold</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Korean share of the market</th><th>Markets</th>
            {아래.byRank.map((x) => <th>#{x.rank}</th>)}
            <th>All places</th><th>#1 minus all</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr class={b.key === 'over20' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{b.label}</td>
              <td>{b.markets}</td>
              {b.byRank.map((x) => <td>{x.pc}</td>)}
              <td class="tot">{b.overallPc}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.gapPp > 0 ? `+${b.gapPp}` : b.gapPp}p</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      All figures are percentages of the places at that rank. Read a row across: in the
      {' '}{중1.markets} markets on the {중1.label} row, the Korean share <i>rises</i> from
      {' '}{중1.rank1Pc}% at number one to {Math.max(...중1.byRank.map((x) => x.pc))}% in the middle of
      the chart. On the bottom row it does the opposite — {위.rank1Pc}% at number one falling to
      {' '}{위.rank10Pc}% at number ten.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The same four rows, drawn</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">
      <b>Each row is scaled to its own largest bar.</b> That is deliberate: the bands sit at very
      different levels, and levels are already in the table above. What is worth seeing here is the
      <i>shape</i> — where in the chart each band peaks.
    </p>
    {data.bands.map((b) => (
      <div class="shape">
        <div class="shape-nm">{b.label} <span class="fine">({b.markets} markets)</span></div>
        <div class="bars">
          {막대(b).map((x) => (
            <div class="bar-cell">
              <div class="bar" style={`height:${x.w}%`} title={`#${x.rank}: ${x.pc}%`}></div>
              <div class="bar-lb">{x.rank}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
    <p class="note">
      The top three rows are humps — Korean titles are commonest in the middle of the chart. The
      bottom row is a slope down from number one. ⚠ Nothing here says one shape is better; a market
      on the bottom row is a market where Korean titles fill a quarter of the chart to begin with.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The objection: is this one title?</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      Number one is a tenth as many places as the chart, so a single long-running hit can make the
      whole shape. It has to be checked before anything else, and in one band it is exactly what is
      happening: in the {아래.markets} markets on the {아래.label} row, <b>{아래.rank1Titles.biggestTitle}</b>
      {' '}alone accounts for {아래.rank1Titles.biggestSharePc}% of Korean number-one weeks. Take out the
      three biggest titles and that row's number-one share falls to
      {' '}<b>{아래.rank1Titles.rank1PcWithoutTopThree}%</b> against {아래.overallPc}% overall.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Korean share of the market</th><th>Korean #1 weeks</th><th>Distinct titles</th>
            <th>Distinct titles per market</th><th>Largest title</th><th>Its share of them</th>
            <th>#1 share without the top three</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr class={b.key === 'over20' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{b.label}</td>
              <td>{b.rank1Titles.koreanWeeks}</td>
              <td>{b.rank1Titles.distinctTitles}</td>
              <td>{b.rank1Titles.distinctPerMarketMean}</td>
              <td class="nm">{b.rank1Titles.biggestTitle}</td>
              <td>{b.rank1Titles.biggestSharePc}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.rank1Titles.rank1PcWithoutTopThree}% <span class="fine">(all: {b.overallPc}%)</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>The objection holds everywhere except the bottom row.</b> There, the average market saw
      {' '}{위.rank1Titles.distinctPerMarketMean} different Korean titles reach number one, the largest
      of them took {위.rank1Titles.biggestSharePc}% of those weeks, and removing the top three still
      leaves {위.rank1Titles.rank1PcWithoutTopThree}% — <i>above</i> the {위.overallPc}% those markets
      give Korean titles overall. That is the one place where topping the chart is routine rather
      than exceptional.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Films and series are separate charts, so we counted them separately</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Korean share of the market</th>
            <th>Films: all places</th><th>Films: #1</th><th>Films: gap</th>
            <th>Series: all places</th><th>Series: #1</th><th>Series: gap</th>
            <th>Markets where #1 is lower</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr class={b.key === 'over20' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{b.label}</td>
              <td>{b.films.overallPc}%</td><td>{b.films.rank1Pc}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.films.gapPp > 0 ? `+${b.films.gapPp}` : b.films.gapPp}p</td>
              <td>{b.tv.overallPc}%</td><td>{b.tv.rank1Pc}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.tv.gapPp > 0 ? `+${b.tv.gapPp}` : b.tv.gapPp}p</td>
              <td>{b.belowAtOne} of {b.markets}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Splitting the two charts does not break the pattern, which is the point of splitting them.
      The last column counts direction rather than size: on the {중1.label} row,
      {' '}{중1.belowAtOne} of {중1.markets} markets have a lower Korean share at number one than
      across the chart. On the bottom row it is {위.belowAtOne} of {위.markets}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The {위.markets} markets on the bottom row</h2>
    <p class="note">
      {위.marketNames.join(', ')}.
      {' '}<b>They are listed alphabetically, not in order of size.</b> We are not ranking them, and
      this list is a description of who is in the band — not a claim about why.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/rank-shape" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix and Wikidata</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>A rank is an outcome, not an explanation.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Bands are cut by measured share, not by geography.</b> A market is on a row because of the
        share we counted for it, so the rows are not regions and should not be read as regions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Every rank has the same number of places.</b> {data.slotsTotal.toLocaleString()} places
        across {data.markets} markets, a tenth of them at each rank, which is why the comparison is
        possible at all.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/catalogue-depth">How few titles fill half a country's chart</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/where-it-moved">Where Korean titles gained and lost</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>

<style>
  .shape { margin: 1.1rem 0 1.4rem; }
  .shape-nm { font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .bars { display: flex; align-items: flex-end; gap: 4px; height: 96px; }
  .bar-cell { flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: flex-end; height: 100%; }
  .bar { background: currentColor; opacity: 0.55; border-radius: 2px 2px 0 0; min-height: 2px; }
  .bar-lb { text-align: center; font-size: 0.7rem; opacity: 0.6; margin-top: 3px; }
</style>
